01|ingest-example|a-m1-p1|examples/a-m1-p1/main.rs|9|16||rows 344 -> 342 OK|read_penguins|assert_eq|The first example loads Palmer Penguins, applies the missing-value policy, and asserts three hundred forty-four rows become three hundred forty-two.|Cargo runs it live. Rows loaded three forty-four, rows kept three forty-two, and the contract line closes the run.
02|profile-example|a-m1-p1-profile|examples/a-m1-p1-profile/main.rs|46|48||contract: ads-rust-p1|max_abs_diff|assert!|The second example prints a per-species profile as JSON, then asserts the largest gap from the golden file is under one in a billion.|Cargo runs it live. Each species prints its means, and the contract line closes the run: every number matches the committed golden file.
03|unit-tests|a-m1-p1|tests/p1.rs|31|46|cargo test --locked --offline --test p1 && cargo run -q --locked --offline --example a-m1-p1|rows 344 -> 342 OK|fn |assert|Now the unit tests for problem one. They check golden parity, and they include mutants: tests that must fail when the code is deliberately wrong. A test suite that cannot fail proves nothing.|The mutants are the point. A test that cannot fail on broken code is not a test.
07|shacl|a-m1-p1|ontology/shapes/example.shape.ttl|1|23|cd "${ADS_ROOT:?}" && make gate-shacl|shacl OK|shacl|OK|Next the SHACL check. Apache Jena validates the example data against the shapes in the repository, so a malformed record is refused before it reaches the code.|Bad data is refused at the door, before it can reach the model.
09|rust-gate|a-m1-p1|scripts/gate-rust.sh|1|13|cd "${ADS_ROOT:?}" && make gate-rust|rust gate OK|clippy|llvm-cov|This is the Rust gate: formatting, clippy with warnings denied, one hundred percent line and function coverage, and every example printing its contract line. The gate scenes close each module. The same four gates guard every problem in this course, so a green run here means the whole pipeline is reproducible.|Green means formatted, lint-free, fully covered, and every example proving its contract.
